idative stress products  and the hope is maybe it could predict  radiation pneumonitis  and we are planning larger validation  studies in humans  however of course i must emphasize at  this stage this is for research use only  it is not an fda approved test  now this is the point in my presentation  where usually i can hear members of the  audience stirring restively  and saying well if breath tests are so  good  why isn't everybody using them already  well there's a reason for it i have  represented a breath test as a leaping  lion here  this is the men's sound of breath says  and that leaping lion wants to get its  jaws into this juicy piece of meat here  the market but in order for the breath  test to reach the market  it has to jump through free flaming  hoops  the first two is to demonstrate  scientific validity which we've done  the second hoop is to get fda approval  which we haven't  and the third step is to get medicare  reimbursement which you can't get  without  fda approval  so this process by the way is much  easier outside the united states  because there are much lower regulatory  barriers in the european union  and we are very close to marketing the  breadth test for breast cancer  in europe and indeed we have three  studies commencing there as we speak  so here is where we stand with breath  testing we have a very long product  pipeline  some of them closer to fruition than  others and you'll see that we've  included lung cancer  tuberculosis breast cancer influenza  counter-terrorism  we've got many tests in the pipeline  some  closer than others and we do hope the  cancer test will get there first  so thank you for listening so patiently  and i hope you will agree with me  quickly  that this is this is taking take  economic technology  oh we've got a few we've got a few  minutes left we're asking for questions  thank you michael for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  so let's get started michael our first  question is  how can a breath test tell the  difference between lung cancer  and breast cancer if they both use  biomarkers of oxidative stress  very good question and the reasoning  behind this  is that people have assumed for the  longest time that oxidative stress was  just one simple procedure that is you  were got it and  generated a number of metabolites what  we've discovered over the years in the  course of all this research  is that oxidative stress is a highly  modulated  process so that even though we're  invoking  oxidative stress let's say in breast  cancer and in  lung cancer we do see a bunch of  metabolites and biomarkers which  are consistent with oxidative stress  products but they are distributed in a  totally different fashion  so the pattern of these products which  are not just alkanes but also  methylated alkanes and alkane  derivatives even though the rules  derived from oxidative stress come out  in a totally different pattern  so that the algorithms we derive let's  say for lung cancer  are totally different from those that we  see in breast cancer  and we see this right across the board  even with infections and radiation  even though yes we ascribe the outcome  to oxidative stress  the patterns are completely different in  every disease that we look at  great thank you so much so moving on to  our next question  might breast test be used for other  cancers such as gi tract cancers or  perhaps  liver or pancreatic cancers  the answer to that is absolutely yes  there's a very large literature on this  it mainly comes from european  researchers as well  but indeed there have been a number of  pilot studies in humans  showing positive results in gi tract  cancers  certainly pancreatic cancers as well  so and in head and neck cancers so  there's been a lot of research ongoing  in this a lot of publications  and the answer is definitely yes  wonderful we do want to thank our  audience for all these questions coming  in today  so moving on to our next one now can  breath tests  detect infections like covid  once again the answer is definitely yes  we haven't studied covert  in our lab but a number of other  researchers have done so  and they've reported some very positive  encouraging results with it  we have looked at patients with  infer infections such as viral influenza  and we've definitely seen biomarkers of  viral influenza and also of  um coronaviruses as well but we have not  looked at  coverts specifically but the answer is  definitely yes it could potentially be  used  for covet screening wonderful  so here we we received a similar  question to this  um a moment ago um in regards to you  know are there other opportunities  um for detecting different types of  cancer  so with this next question is you know  which cancers can we detect by breath  right now and  you know what about the specificity of  cancer detection  well uh let's address that question of  specificity  that's exactly what we look at in the  rock curve because you see that is  a plot of sensitivity versus specificity  so that we know that if you pick the  sweet spot on that rock curve  you can get a very good combination of  sensitivity and specificity  you can make that test 100 specific but  then you  there's a trade-off you lose some  sensitivity vice versa  you can make the test 100 or close to it  sensitive but then you lose some  specificity  this is just the trade-off that you see  whenever you've got a cancer test or  indeed any test where you can display  the results on a rock curve  and now in what way do the metabolites  of environmental pollutants  can they interfere with the breast test  for cancer  done quite a bit of work with  environmental pollutants as have many  other people  this was pioneered by the environmental  protection agency some years ago back in  the 60s and 70s  and they showed in very large studies  around the united states  that a breath test can pick up lots of  environmental pollutants  particularly btex compounds such as  benzene  and toluene etc and  it's a very sensitive test for exposure  to environmental pollutants  now the question really focuses here  does it  interfere with the tests for cancer or  other diseases and the answer is yes it  could  provide but it doesn't if you have a  really  good analytical method that is why we  use  mass spectrometry and if you tune your  method really right  you can tune out the interfering  compounds so that it doesn't overlap  with the biomarkers  it is a threat you know there is a  potential  for it to happen but if you're very  careful with the analytic technique  you can make sure that the peaks for  various compounds such as benzene and  toluene  come out at different places from the  biomarkers  thank you so much for that clarification  so moving on to  our next question so dr phillips how  reproducible can this technique be and  the second part of the question is could  it be marketed as a turnkey lab  well take the second part first yes of  course it could  how reproducible well we did a very  careful reproducibility study  if you recall the slide i shared about  lung cancer  we took duplicate samples from something  like 500 patients  and analyzed them in two separate  laboratories  now of course both laboratories were  using the same technology they were both  using  gc mass spectrometry and we went to a  lot of trouble before the study started  to make sure that these instruments had  similar  responses to compounds but you saw  when we analyzed similar split samples  from two uh from subjects 500 subjects  and we put them into two different  laboratories the results were remarkably  similar  the rock curves just about overlapped  each other  great thank you so much so we do have  time for a few more questions  but i do want to remind our audience  that any questions that we're unable to  answer today  and those that come in during the  on-demand period you know will be  addressed by our speaker  via the email address that you provided  at the time of registration  so please keep those questions coming in  as we're getting some really great ones  from the audience right now  so moving on to our next one are there  ratios of compounds that could act as  anchors for  interlab reproducibility  tough one that uh simply because human  beings are not all that reproducible  for interlab reproducibility  we can use the method we choose is to  use either an  internal standard or an external  standard  so for instance with our lab  gc mass spec system we have a sampling  loop at the front end  which takes in a standard amount of our  internal standard we use  bromofluorobenzene  and the reason we picked the  bromofluorobenzene is that you don't see  it in the breath you don't see it in the  air  and when you use that you can calibrate  it and so get to enhance your interlab  reproducibility  great thank you so much um  looks like we've got maybe one more  question that we have time for  um so dr phillips does the breath  test for lung cancer change after the  cancer is removed  surgically uh we have some very  interesting pilot data on this  in one of our earlier lung cancer  studies we  re we repeated the breath test  about a month after the patients had  undergone surgery in approximately 30  patients and we were astonished to find  that the breath signal did not change  after surgery well of course the  surgeons were very disappointed when  they heard that they thought we had a  test to see if they got all the cancer  out but i think this is telling us  something  very profound about the nature of cancer  and the  biological response to cancer in that we  are  probably just based on this very limited  subset of patients  probably not seeing a signal coming from  the tumor itself  or if it is it's not the whole story  because if that signal persists after  the tumor  is removed then we're seeing a much more  complicated background story  of what's the biochemical changes in the  body so  this is a very fruitful field for future  research  early days yet but i think it's a very  exciting area to explore further  wonderful thank you so much michael for  for your presentation and answering all  those questions  do you have any final comments for our  audience  well uh first of all thank you all very  much for tuning in to this and listening  and i know that not everybody has had  any had an opportunity to have their  questions answered  so if you have any questions please feel  free to drop me an email  and i'll do my best to answer them for  you  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions that we did not have time for  today and those submitted during the  on-demand period  will be addressed by our speaker via the  contact information  that you provided at the time of  registration we would like to once again  thank dr michael phillips for his time  today and for his important research  we would also like to thank labyrinth  and our sponsor agilent  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go um you can view the webinar  on demand  laborats will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  that's all for now thank you again for  joining us and until next time  stay safe stay healthy bye bye everyone
 

